@page "/docs/interactivity/alpine-code-behind"
@namespace RizzyUI.Docs.Components.Pages.Interactivity

@using RizzyUI
@using RizzyUI.Docs.Components.Pages.Interactivity.Examples

<PageTitle>Your First Alpine Code-Behind</PageTitle>

<RzQuickReferenceContainer>
    <RzArticle ProseWidth="ProseWidth.UltraWide">
        <SideContent>
            <RzQuickReference />
        </SideContent>
        <MainContent>
            <RzBreadcrumb class="mb-4 not-prose">
                <BreadcrumbList>
                    <BreadcrumbItem>
                        <BreadcrumbLink Href="/" hx-boost="true" hx-select="#content" hx-target="#content" hx-swap="outerHTML">Docs</BreadcrumbLink>
                    </BreadcrumbItem>
                    <BreadcrumbSeparator />
                    <BreadcrumbItem>
                        Interactivity
                    </BreadcrumbItem>
                    <BreadcrumbSeparator />
                    <BreadcrumbItem>
                        <BreadcrumbPage>Your First Code-Behind</BreadcrumbPage>
                    </BreadcrumbItem>
                </BreadcrumbList>
            </RzBreadcrumb>
            <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H1" QuickReferenceTitle="Your First Alpine Code-Behind">
                Your First Alpine Code-Behind
            </RzHeading>

            <RzParagraph class="lead">
                Give a component some browser-side behavior by putting its JavaScript in a file right next to it:
                <code>Counter.razor</code> holds the markup, <code>Counter.razor.js</code> holds the state and the
                methods. Let's build a counter.
            </RzParagraph>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="1. The markup">
                    1. The markup
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    Mark the component with <code>[RzAlpineCodeBehind]</code> and wrap its markup in
                    <code>&lt;RzAlpineComponent For="this"&gt;</code>. Inside, use Alpine as usual: <code>x-text</code>
                    shows a value, <code>x-on:click</code> calls a method.
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.Razor" ViewerTitle="Counter.razor" class="mt-4">
@@attribute [RzAlpineCodeBehind]

&lt;RzAlpineComponent For=&quot;this&quot; AsChild&gt;
  &lt;div class=&quot;flex items-center gap-4&quot;&gt;
    &lt;RzButton x-on:click=&quot;decrement&quot;&gt;-&lt;/RzButton&gt;
    &lt;span x-text=&quot;count&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;RzButton x-on:click=&quot;increment&quot;&gt;+&lt;/RzButton&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/RzAlpineComponent&gt;
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzParagraph class="mt-4">
                    <code>AsChild</code> puts the component on the <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> itself instead of adding a
                    wrapper around it.
                </RzParagraph>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="2. The JavaScript">
                    2. The JavaScript
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    In <code>Counter.razor.js</code>, export a function that returns the component's state and methods.
                    The names match what the markup uses.
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.JavaScript" ViewerTitle="Counter.razor.js" class="mt-4">
export default () =&gt; ({
  count: 0,

  increment() {
    this.count++;
  },

  decrement() {
    this.count--;
  },
});
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzParagraph class="mt-4">
                    That's it: put <code>&lt;Counter /&gt;</code> on a page and it works, including in content that htmx
                    swaps in later. To create both files in one step, run
                    <code>dotnet new rizzyui-codebehind -n Counter</code>.
                </RzParagraph>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="3. Passing data from C#">
                    3. Passing data from C#
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    Pass values with <code>Props</code> and read them in JavaScript with <code>this.$props</code>, using
                    the same names you wrote in C#.
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzBrowser Layout="typeof(PreviewLayout)">
                    <div class="mx-auto flex items-center justify-center gap-4 p-8 min-h-20">
                        <CounterWithProps />
                    </div>
                </RzBrowser>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.Razor" ViewerTitle="CounterWithProps.razor" class="mt-4">
@@attribute [RzAlpineCodeBehind]

&lt;RzAlpineComponent For=&quot;this&quot; AsChild Props=&quot;@@(new { InitialCount = 10, Step = 2 })&quot;&gt;
  &lt;div class=&quot;flex items-center gap-4&quot;&gt;
    &lt;RzButton x-on:click=&quot;decrement&quot;&gt;-&lt;/RzButton&gt;
    &lt;span x-text=&quot;count&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;RzButton x-on:click=&quot;increment&quot;&gt;+&lt;/RzButton&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/RzAlpineComponent&gt;
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.JavaScript" ViewerTitle="CounterWithProps.razor.js" class="mt-4">
export default () =&gt; ({
  count: 0,
  step: 1,

  init() {
    this.count = this.$props.InitialCount;
    this.step = this.$props.Step;
  },

  increment() {
    this.count += this.step;
  },

  decrement() {
    this.count -= this.step;
  },
});
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzParagraph class="mt-4">
                    <code>init()</code> runs once when the component starts, which makes it the place to set up state.
                    In markup you can read props directly too: <code>x-text="$props.InitialCount"</code>.
                </RzParagraph>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="4. Working with components inside yours">
                    4. Working with components inside yours
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    To control a RizzyUI component inside yours, such as opening a dialog, get it in <code>ready()</code>.
                    It runs after <code>init()</code>, once the components inside yours have started.
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.JavaScript" ViewerTitle="ModalController.razor.js" class="mt-4">
export default () =&gt; ({
  dialog: null,

  ready() {
    this.dialog = Rizzy.$data(this.$refs.myDialog);   // &lt;RzDialog x-ref=&quot;myDialog&quot;&gt;
  },

  open() {
    this.dialog.openModal();
  },
});
                </RzCodeViewer>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Editor support">
                    Editor support
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    Add two comment lines at the top of a module and your editor completes <code>this.$props</code>,
                    Alpine's helpers and the <code>Rizzy</code> global, and points out typos:
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.JavaScript" ViewerTitle="CounterWithProps.razor.js" class="mt-4">
// @@ts-check
/** @@type {import('rizzyui').AlpineCodeBehind&lt;{ InitialCount: number, Step: number }&gt;} */
export default () =&gt; ({
  // ...
});
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzParagraph class="mt-4">
                    The starter template is set up for this. In other projects, see
                    <a href="#behind-the-magic-types" class="underline underline-offset-4">Types</a> below.
                </RzParagraph>
            </section>

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="If something doesn't work">
                    If something doesn't work
                </RzHeading>
                <ul class="list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 my-6 text-foreground">
                    <li><strong>Build warning <code>RZUI1001</code>:</strong> the <code>.razor.js</code> file is missing or misnamed. It must be <code>Counter.razor.js</code>, next to <code>Counter.razor</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>An error mentions <code>[RzAlpineCodeBehind]</code>:</strong> add <code>@@attribute [RzAlpineCodeBehind]</code> to the component you pass in <code>For</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>A prop is <code>undefined</code>:</strong> check its name. <code>this.$props</code> uses the names exactly as written in <code>Props</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>A child component's data is <code>undefined</code>:</strong> read it in <code>ready()</code>, not <code>init()</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>The console says <em>Could not load the code-behind module</em>:</strong> open the URL from the message in the browser to see why it fails.</li>
                </ul>
                <RzParagraph>
                    Next: reactivity, <code>$watch</code> and stores in
                    <RzLink Href="/docs/interactivity/state-reactivity">Working with State and Reactivity</RzLink>.
                </RzParagraph>
            </section>

            <RzSeparator />

            <section class="my-8 py-2">
                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H2" QuickReferenceTitle="Behind the Magic" Id="behind-the-magic">
                    Behind the Magic
                </RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    You don't need any of this to write components. It is here for when you want to know exactly what
                    happens, or need to change it.
                </RzParagraph>

                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H3" QuickReferenceTitle="Finding the module" class="mt-8">Finding the module</RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    <code>[RzAlpineCodeBehind]</code> records the path of the component's source file at compile time
                    (with <code>[CallerFilePath]</code>), and RizzyUI's build targets record the project folder and the
                    static web asset base path. Together they give the module's URL: the path of
                    <code>Counter.razor.js</code> in the project, for example <code>/Features/Counter.razor.js</code>, or
                    <code>/_content/MyLibrary/Counter.razor.js</code> in a Razor class library. The attribute can also go
                    on the class in <code>Counter.razor.cs</code>. The <code>.razor.js</code> file is published as a static
                    web asset exactly as written.
                </RzParagraph>

                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H3" QuickReferenceTitle="What the page receives" class="mt-8">What the page receives</RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    <code>RzAlpineComponent</code> renders only attributes on the component's root element, no scripts:
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.Html" class="mt-4">
&lt;div id=&quot;rzX1&quot; data-alpine-root=&quot;rzX1&quot;
     x-data=&quot;rzcb_CounterWithProps_1a2b3c4d&quot;
     x-load=&quot;eager&quot;
     x-load-src=&quot;/Features/CounterWithProps.ezjsubrzj2.razor.js&quot;
     data-rz-module-fallback=&quot;/Features/CounterWithProps.razor.js&quot;
     data-rz-props='{&quot;InitialCount&quot;:10,&quot;Step&quot;:2}'&gt;
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 my-6 text-foreground">
                    <li><code>x-load</code> and <code>x-load-src</code> are read by async-alpine, which RizzyUI includes: it imports the module when the load strategy allows (<code>eager</code> by default; <code>LoadStrategy</code> can be <code>visible</code>, <code>idle</code>, <code>media (…)</code> or <code>event</code>), registers its default export under the <code>x-data</code> name, and starts the element.</li>
                    <li>Because nothing is inline, the same markup works in the first page load, in htmx swaps, and under a Content Security Policy without <code>'unsafe-inline'</code> or nonces per component.</li>
                    <li>Components that load eagerly also render <code>&lt;link rel="modulepreload"&gt;</code> once per request, so the browser fetches the module while it reads the page. <code>Preload="false"</code> turns it off.</li>
                </ul>

                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H3" QuickReferenceTitle="The x-data name" class="mt-8">The <em>x-data</em> name</RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    The name is generated from the component type and a hash of the module's URL. Two components can never
                    collide, and when a deployment changes a module, its URL (and so its name) changes, so a page left open
                    loads the new module for new markup instead of reusing the old one. Set <code>Name</code> only when
                    other markup refers to the component by name; RizzyUI warns if two modules use the same name.
                </RzParagraph>

                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H3" QuickReferenceTitle="Props" class="mt-8">Props</RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    <code>Props</code> is serialized with <code>System.Text.Json</code> into <code>data-rz-props</code>,
                    keeping the property names written in C#. <code>this.$props</code> (and <code>Rizzy.props(el)</code>)
                    parse it once and return a shared, read-only object; copy values into the component's state to change
                    them. Inside nested code-behind components, <code>$props</code> always belongs to the nearest one.
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzParagraph>
                    The serializer can be configured for the whole app, for example to use camelCase names (the convention of
                    Blazor's JavaScript interop) or to send enums as names:
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.CSharp" ViewerTitle="Program.cs" class="mt-4">
builder.Services.AddRizzyUI(config =&gt;
{
    config.CodeBehindPropsSerializerOptions = new JsonSerializerOptions(JsonSerializerDefaults.Web);   // camelCase
    config.CodeBehindPropsSerializerOptions.Converters.Add(new JsonStringEnumConverter());
});
                </RzCodeViewer>
                <RzParagraph class="mt-4">
                    To rename a single property, use <code>[JsonPropertyName]</code>.
                </RzParagraph>

                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H3" QuickReferenceTitle="init() and ready()" class="mt-8"><em>init()</em> and <em>ready()</em></RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    <code>init()</code> is Alpine's own hook: it runs when the component starts, before the components inside
                    it, so state you set there is on screen from the first render. RizzyUI components load their code on
                    demand, so a dialog inside yours may not have started yet. When the module defines <code>ready()</code>,
                    RizzyUI calls it after <code>init()</code> (and after its promise, if <code>init()</code> is
                    <code>async</code>) and after every component inside yours that loads eagerly has started. Components
                    with a lazy strategy (<code>visible</code>, <code>event</code>, ...) are not waited for. If something
                    takes longer than three seconds, <code>ready()</code> runs anyway and the console says which components
                    were still loading.
                </RzParagraph>

                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H3" QuickReferenceTitle="Types" class="mt-8" Id="behind-the-magic-types">Types</RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    RizzyUI ships TypeScript declarations for modules. The NuGet package copies them to
                    <code>obj/rizzyui/rizzyui.d.ts</code> on every build; a <code>jsconfig.json</code> maps the name
                    <code>rizzyui</code> to that file (the npm package exports the same file as
                    <code>@@jalexsocial/rizzyui</code>):
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzCodeViewer Language="@CodeLanguage.Json" ViewerTitle="jsconfig.json" class="mt-4">
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "paths": { "rizzyui": ["./obj/rizzyui/rizzyui.d.ts"] }
  },
  "include": ["**/*.razor.js", "obj/rizzyui/rizzyui.d.ts"]
}
                </RzCodeViewer>

                <RzHeading Level="HeadingLevel.H3" QuickReferenceTitle="When a module fails to load" class="mt-8">When a module fails to load</RzHeading>
                <RzParagraph>
                    If a fingerprinted URL fails (typically a page older than the current deployment), RizzyUI retries once
                    with the plain URL. If that fails too, it logs one error naming the component and the URL, dispatches
                    <code>rz:module-error</code> on <code>document</code>, and gives the element an empty component so the
                    rest of the page keeps working.
                </RzParagraph>
                <RzParagraph>
                    Caching, deployments, CSP, minification, TypeScript modules and testing are covered in
                    <RzLink Href="/docs/interactivity/code-behind-deployment">Building and Deploying Code-Behind</RzLink>.
                </RzParagraph>
            </section>

        </MainContent>
    </RzArticle>
</RzQuickReferenceContainer>
